+# Specialty-Filtered Veterinarian Reads Bypass the Vet Cache
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+`VetRepository`'s two unfiltered read methods both carry `@Cacheable("vets")`. Their keys form a closed set: the no-argument read keys on nothing, and the paged read keys on the page request, so the entry count is bounded by the number of pages.
+
+`REQ-VET-004` adds reads narrowed by a specialty the caller names in a query parameter. Caching those with the same annotation would key entries on caller-supplied text. The `vets` cache has no eviction configured, and the application has no write path for veterinarians — recorded as an open question in [`system-design.md`](../system-design.md#open-questions-from-the-survey). An evictionless cache keyed on unbounded caller input grows without limit while any caller keeps naming fresh values.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Annotate the filtered reads `@Cacheable("vets")`** — uniform with the sibling methods. Every distinct string a caller sends becomes a permanent entry.
+2. **Give the filtered reads a separate cache with a size bound and an eviction policy** — keeps the hit rate and caps the growth. Needs a second cache declaration in `CacheConfiguration` and a JCache provider configuration this project does not carry today.
+3. **Leave the filtered reads uncached** (chosen) — no new cache surface, no unbounded keys.
+
+## Decision
+
+The specialty-filtered read methods on `VetRepository` carry no `@Cacheable` annotation. The unfiltered reads keep theirs unchanged.
+
+The filtered query is a single indexed join across three small tables, so the read cost it avoids is small. Option 2 buys a hit rate this workload does not need and adds cache configuration whose eviction question is already open. Option 1 turns a query parameter into an unbounded memory allocation, which the resource-limit bar in [`security-principles.md`](../security-principles.md#realization) does not admit.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Every narrowed request reaches the database. Unnarrowed requests still serve from the cache.
+- No caller-supplied text becomes a cache key, so the cache's entry count stays bounded by the page count.
+- The `vets` cache keeps exactly the eviction question it had before this requirement; the open question is neither widened nor answered.
+- A later measurement showing the filtered read to be hot reopens this as option 2, with a bound and a policy, rather than by adding the annotation.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Requirements:** REQ-VET-004
+
+## References
+
+- [system-design.md — Contracts](../system-design.md#contracts) — the `VetRepository` row states which reads are cached.
+- [system-design.md — Open Questions](../system-design.md#open-questions-from-the-survey) — the standing vet cache invalidation question.
